Unmute: HackUMass XIV Team Handoff
Nov 13, 2026 · @Someone
Introduction
We are building Unmute, a silent speech interface that lets patients who cannot make a sound talk again. A camera reads their lips, AI turns the lip movement into a full sentence, and the laptop speaks it out loud.
Who it is for. Patients on a ventilator in the ICU are often awake and alert, but the breathing tube stops them from speaking. Today, nurses guess what they are mouthing or hand them a letter board. People who lost their voice to throat cancer surgery or ALS face the same problem.
One-line pitch. "Unmute gives a voice back to patients who cannot speak, using only a camera."
Inspiration. At TreeHacks 2025, BlinkAI won Best Beginner by turning blinked Morse code into text. Morse code is slow and exhausting. We read whole sentences from the lips instead.
Why it is unique
The problem is proven, and the closest product is limited. Our angle is open sentences, context and working offline.
Honest prior art. A Belfast company, Liopa, built SRAVI, a lip-reading app for tracheostomy patients that recognises a fixed list of 40 phrases, such as "I need the toilet." It has been tested in UK hospitals, and one feasibility study reported about 75% accuracy. A UK study of its acceptability found the main complaints were the effort to use it, and Wi-Fi problems. Judges may know it, so we say this up front.

