Is it doable
Yes, because we do not train a lip reader from scratch. We use an open-source one and build the product around it.
• The model exists. Auto-AVSR is an open-source lip-reading model with a word error rate of about 20% on its benchmark. Copies of the weights are on Hugging Face, for example this re-host, which reports 25 to 30% on clean speech.
• The license. The weights are for non-commercial research use. That is fine for a hackathon. Say so if a judge asks about the business.
• Hardware. A laptop webcam. Inference on a short clip should take a few seconds on a laptop CPU. A GPU is not required.
Risk
What we do about it
Setup of the model eats the night
One person only. Hard stop at 3 AM: if no words appear on screen, Quick Phrases becomes the core.
The raw model gets 1 in 4 words wrong
Gemini fixes words using bedside context, then the patient picks from 3 choices.
Lip reading fails on our faces or lighting
Good front light, camera at face height, mouth clearly visible. Calibrate on each speaker.
The live demo fails at judging
Quick Phrases mode is trained on our own faces and should be near-perfect. Record a backup video Saturday evening.
Many words look the same on the lips
Expected. That is why we show 3 choices instead of one guess.
How it works
Open sentences go through the lip reader and Gemini. Quick Phrases skip both and go straight to the choices, which keeps the demo safe.
