Features to add later
Add these in order, and only once the core works end to end. Each one answers a weakness a judge might raise.
Priority
Feature
Why it matters
1
Per-patient calibration: the patient mouths 10 known phrases, and the model adapts to their lips
Every face is different. This is the biggest accuracy gain.
2
Bedside context: time of day, last meal, who is in the room, recent requests go into the Gemini prompt
"I need wader" becomes "I need water" because it has been 3 hours since a drink.
3
Confirm by nod: head pose picks choice 1, 2 or 3, a head shake means none
Many patients cannot tap a screen.
4
Offline mode: lip reading runs on the laptop, Gemini is only used when online
Wi-Fi problems were a top complaint about the existing app.
5
Accuracy report: test on held-out clips and show the word error rate before and after Gemini correction
Real numbers beat claims. Most teams never measure.
6
Nurse dashboard: a log of what each patient asked for and when
Shows the product fits a real ward, not just a demo.
7
Their own voice: patients record their voice before surgery, and Unmute speaks in it
A strong emotional moment for the pitch. Only if time allows.
8
Other languages
Auto-AVSR has models for several languages. A "next steps" line in the pitch.
Demo, pitch and prizes
The demo moment: a judge mouths a sentence in silence, and the laptop says it out loud.
Two-minute pitch
1.  Hook (15 s): "Imagine waking up in the ICU, fully alert, and you cannot make a sound."
2.  Problem (15 s): breathing tubes take away the voice. Letter boards are slow, and the existing app only knows 40 phrases.
3.  Live demo (60 s): a teammate mouths "I need water" in Quick Phrases mode. Then a judge mouths an open sentence, the screen shows 3 choices, a nod picks one, and it is spoken.
4.  Proof (15 s): our word error rate before and after Gemini correction.
5.  Vision (15 s): per-patient calibration, offline on any tablet, their own voice.
